      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Is Remotion free or paid?</div><div class="a">Both, depending on who you are. The free license covers individuals, non-profits, for-profit organizations of up to three people, and anyone evaluating it. Above that, published pricing lists Remotion for Creators at $25 per seat per month and Remotion for Automators at $0.01 per render with a $100 monthly minimum. Enterprise starts at $500 a month.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Do you need a Remotion license to make videos for a client?</div><div class="a">Possibly, and the headcount rule is stricter than it first appears. Remotion's license FAQ states that freelancers, another studio, or a consulting agency helping operate the software on the same project aggregate with your own headcount toward the four-person threshold. A small team plus contractors can cross the line without hiring anyone.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">How do you install Claude Code skills from GitHub?</div><div class="a">Either drop the skill folder into your skills directory, or install it through the agent's plugin system. Remotion's official skills install with <code>npx skills add remotion-dev/skills</code>. Its Claude Code plugin installs with <code>claude plugin marketplace add remotion-dev/claude-code-plugin</code> then <code>claude plugin install remotion@remotion</code>, followed by a restart.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">What is an agent skill, and how is it different from an agent?</div><div class="a">An agent skill is a folder of instructions, and sometimes scripts, that an AI agent loads when a task matches it. The agent is the thing that reasons and runs commands. The skill is the knowledge it picks up for a specific job. Best Answer Hub sells skill packs of exactly this kind at the AI Agent Skills hub.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Which AI agents work with Remotion, or is it Claude Code only?</div><div class="a">Not Claude Code only, despite most coverage. Remotion's own documentation lists Claude Code, Codex, Kimi Code and Cursor as supported agents for its skills and plugins. The underlying work is writing React and running a render command, so any agent that can edit files and run a terminal is a candidate.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">How much does it cost to make an AI video with an agent?</div><div class="a">For one person using a local voice and the free Remotion license, the software cost is zero. The floors above that, from published August 2026 prices, are $6 a month for a commercially licensed cloud voice, $18 a month for a hosted video agent, and a $100 monthly minimum once a team of four or more automates rendering.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">How do you add a voice-over to an AI video?</div><div class="a">With a local speech model or a cloud text-to-speech API. Kokoro-82M is the common local choice: Apache 2.0, 54 voices, and fast enough on a processor that one project measured it at roughly five times real time. Cloud options sound excellent but add a per-use cost and a license to check.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Can you use the ElevenLabs free plan for a monetized video?</div><div class="a">No. ElevenLabs states that its free plan does not include a commercial license and cannot be used for any commercial purpose. The cheapest tier that grants one is Starter at $6 a month. An Apache 2.0 local model such as Kokoro-82M has no equivalent restriction, which is why license-aware pipelines default to running the voice locally.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Why does the narration drift out of sync with the visuals?</div><div class="a">Because text-to-speech does not deliver a predictable speaking rate, so a script timed for 50 seconds can return 40 to 45 seconds of audio. One toolkit reports roughly 30% drift on a five-second scene and 10% on a thirty-second one. The fix is structural: generate the audio first, then cue every visual to real word timestamps.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">How long does it take to render an AI-generated video?</div><div class="a">Longer than the video itself, usually by a multiple. Frames are drawn one at a time, so 30 seconds at 30 frames per second means 900 renders. One production user reported roughly two to four times the video's length on an 8-core cloud machine. Blur, shadows and gradients are the most expensive effects.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Can AI edit my existing video footage?</div><div class="a">Partly, and this is the weakest area. A practitioner who published a complete agent video workflow describes editing existing footage as decent but far from perfect, with rough transitions. Code-rendered pipelines are built to compose new motion graphics, not to cut raw footage, and natural-language footage editing is a separate category of tool.</div></div>
      <div class="qa"><div class="q">Will YouTube demonetize AI-generated videos?</div><div class="a">Not for using AI, but for the pattern careless automation produces. YouTube's monetization policy disallows AI-generated content made with generic or unoriginal templates that gives the impression of mass production without the creator's own insight, and requires that the substance of each video is materially varied. Low volume with a human in the loop is what stays compliant.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="cav">Where these numbers came from</div>
    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      <li><a href="https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2025/11/20/americans-social-media-use-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pew Research Center, Americans' Social Media Use 2025</a> (20 November 2025, n = 5,022 US adults): 84% of US adults ever use YouTube.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.bls.gov/ooh/media-and-communication/film-and-video-editors-and-camera-operators.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">US Bureau of Labor Statistics, Occupational Outlook Handbook</a> (page modified 27 August 2026): median annual wage for film and video editors, $75,420, May 2025.</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/remotion-dev/remotion/blob/main/LICENSE.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Remotion license</a> and <a href="https://www.remotion.dev/docs/license/faq" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Remotion license FAQ</a>: the three-employee free tier, the contractor aggregation clause, and the Remotion 5.0 notice.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.remotion.pro/license" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Remotion pricing</a>: Creators $25 per seat per month, Automators $0.01 per render with a $100 monthly minimum, Enterprise from $500 a month.</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/remotion-dev/remotion/issues/4783" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Remotion issue #4783</a>: a production user's reported render time of roughly two to four times the input video length.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.remotion.dev/docs/performance" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Remotion performance documentation</a>: blur, shadows and gradients named as GPU-expensive.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.remotion.dev/docs/ai/skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Remotion Agent Skills</a> and <a href="https://www.remotion.dev/docs/ai/claude-code-plugin" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the Claude Code plugin</a>: install commands and the supported agent list.</li>
      <li><a href="https://huggingface.co/hexgrad/Kokoro-82M" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kokoro-82M model card</a>: Apache 2.0 license, 54 voices, 8 languages, 82 million parameters.</li>
